## Context

`week3_capable` in the classmate's repo
(`claude-code-camp-2026-Q2-htnguyen901`) is that student's answer to the
camp's Week 3 assignment, whose rubric already sits, unfilled, in **this**
repo at `docs/journal/3_capable.md`:

> Design the Agentic Loop that is capable of executing complex goals — plan
> decomposition, refined memory/knowledge access, (optional) playstyle/risk
> mode.

This repo has done Week 0 (`week0_explore`), Week 1 (`week1_baseline`, steps
`00_config`…`12_context`), and Week 2 (`week2_observability`, Phases A–F:
navigation debugging, the `mud_monitor` log viewer, deterministic room
survey, and — importantly — **automatic room memory already exists**:
`Mud::Hooks` injects a `[here]...` state block every turn from a
SQLite-backed room graph
(`boukensha/lib/boukensha/mud/memory/{schema,store}.rb`), with a `/knowledge`
+ `/knowledge/map` viewer in `mud_monitor`. This repo's own `ITERATIONS.md`
roadmap explicitly flags what's *not* built yet: **"An orchestrator layer...
no orchestrator, turn-counter wrapper, or memory-store code exists"** (the
memory-store note is now stale — Phase D built that — but **no orchestrator
exists**, and no *cross-session* character memory exists either).

**Goal of this plan:** reach the same "capable" milestone
(orchestration/planning, richer knowledge access, navigation, memory) — but
by *extending* the existing Phase A–F architecture, not by porting
htnguyen901's files. Where this repo already solved a piece (room memory,
room graph, permissions engine), reuse it; where it's a true gap
(orchestrator, cross-session memory), build it fresh, informed by how the
reference did it.

## What htnguyen901 built (for reference, not for copying)

| Step | Capability | Core mechanism |
|---|---|---|
| `18_orchestrator` | Planner sets a plan, Player runs, Judge checkpoints | `Tasks::Planner`/`Tasks::Judge`, `Context#plan` injected into system prompt, verdict `continue/replan/flag` drives a `Session.play` outer loop |
| `19_knowledge` | World knowledge queryable, not just injected | Moved room/route knowledge into a separate `log_viz --mcp` server (`world__room_knowledge`, `world__route_to` — BFS over an edges table) |
| `20_navigator` | Dedicated path-finding subagent | `Tasks::Navigator`, read-only, bounded, isolated context; exposed to Player/Judge/Planner as a native `consult_navigator` tool |
| `21_memory` | Cross-session character memory | `PlayerMemory` (jsonl + prose digest), `Tasks::Chronicler` (zero tools), reaches the Player only via the Planner's prompt |

Their `Tasks::*` all subclass a `Tasks::Base` that reads `provider`/`model`/
`prompt_override`/`max_iterations` per task name out of `settings.yaml`'s
`tasks:` block — **this repo already has this exact pattern**
(`boukensha/lib/boukensha/tasks/base.rb`, `tasks/player.rb`), so adding
`Tasks::Planner`/`Tasks::Judge`/`Tasks::Navigator`/`Tasks::Chronicler` is a
drop-in, not new plumbing.

## Gap analysis against this repo

| Capability | Here today | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| Room/world memory | **Have it.** `Mud::Memory::Store` (SQLite: rooms, room_exits, entities, entity_sightings, player_state), `Mud::Hooks` auto-injects state, zero LLM calls on known rooms, `/knowledge` + `/knowledge/map` UI. | Reuse as-is. Do not rebuild. |
| Navigation data | **Have the graph.** `room_exits` table, `KnowledgeStore#all_exits` (mud_monitor read side). | Reuse the schema; add a query path + a subagent on top. |
| Query access to knowledge (for tasks other than Player) | **Missing.** Knowledge only ever reaches the Player, only as an auto-injected block — nothing can *ask* "what's at room X" or "is there a path A→B" on demand. | Build (small — a thin read API over the existing `Store`). |
| Orchestrator (Planner/Judge, plan decomposition, verdict loop) | **Missing entirely** — confirmed by this repo's own `ITERATIONS.md`. | Build (the headline piece). |
| Navigator subagent | **Missing** — the data exists, not a bounded subagent or a `consult_navigator` tool. | Build (thin — mostly wiring over existing data). |
| Cross-session character memory (discoveries/mistakes/strategies across runs) | **Missing.** Existing memory is spatial/world state, not narrative digest across sessions. | Build fresh — genuinely new, closest 1:1 to the reference design. |
| Per-task tool permissions | **Have it, unused.** `Boukensha::Permissions` (Phase A) is built, tested, and off. Judge/Navigator are the first tasks that actually *need* a restricted, read-only tool surface. | Turn on for the new tasks — this is the natural moment. |

## Recommended approach

Fork `week2_observability/{boukensha,mud_manager,mud_monitor}` into
`week3_capable/` exactly the way `week2_observability` forked its two
sources (see `week2_observability/README.md`'s "Setup" section) — sibling
gems, cross-package paths repointed, nothing in `week2_observability`
touched. Continue the existing "Phase" lettering (A–F already used) into
**Phases G–J**, one per capability, each with its own
`docs/plans/capability/week3_phase_*.md` write-up (mirroring
`docs/plans/observability/week2_phase_*.md`) plus this file as the rollup
(mirroring `week2_catchup_plan.md`).

### Phase G — Orchestrator: Planner + Judge

- `Tasks::Planner < Tasks::Base` (`task_name = "planner"`, no tools) and
  `Tasks::Judge < Tasks::Base` (`task_name = "judge"`, read-only tools only)
  — same shape as the existing `Tasks::Player`.
- `Context#plan` + `Context#effective_system` — plan text folded into the
  system prompt so it survives `Context#compact_messages!` (Step 12's
  compactor already exists; this just needs to not evict the plan).
- `Boukensha.run_planner` — one bare model call, no loop.
- `Boukensha.run_judge` — a small `Agent#run` with its own throwaway
  `Context`/`Registry`, filtered to `role: inspector` tools (reuse
  `Boukensha::Permissions`, Phase A — this is exactly the restricted-surface
  use case it was built for and never turned on). Parses a trailing
  `VERDICT: continue|replan|flag` line, defaults to `:flag` if unparseable.
- A new outer loop (`Boukensha::Session.play` or an equivalent wrapper
  around the existing `Repl`) drives Planner → Player turns → Judge,
  `max_turns` as an outer backstop. Off by default via
  `tasks.planner.enabled` / `tasks.judge.enabled` in `settings.yaml` (same
  "ship real, opt-in" posture as the Step-12 compactor and Phase A's
  `allow:` engine), flipped on once verified.
- `mud_monitor`'s session transcript (`views/session.erb`) gets per-task
  color coding (Planner/Judge/Player) — additive, same file the trace-link
  work (Phase 3) already extended.

### Phase H — Knowledge as a query API (not a new store)

Do **not** stand up a second process/MCP server the way the reference did
(`log_viz --mcp`) — room memory already lives in-process inside `boukensha`
via `Mud::Memory::Store`, so a network/stdio hop would be pure overhead
here. Instead:

- Add a handful of read-only methods to `Store` (e.g. `all_exits` — the
  batch version `mud_monitor`'s `KnowledgeStore` already proved out for the
  map view, per `docs/plans/observability/week2_phase_d_agent_memory.md`'s
  "Knowledge map" note — port that same batch-query idea into the
  agent-facing `Store`, not just the monitor's read replica) and a
  `route_to(from_id, to_id)` BFS over `room_exits`, scoped to rooms already
  visited (mirrors the reference's own scoping choice).
- Wrap these as one native, read-only tool (register via `RunDSL#tool`,
  dispatch via `RunDSL#dispatch` — the same seam `inspect_room` and
  `Mud::Hooks` already use) available only to Judge/Navigator/Planner's
  registries, gated by `Permissions` — the Player keeps its existing
  zero-tool-call auto-injected state block unchanged.

### Phase I — Navigator subagent

- `Tasks::Navigator < Tasks::Base` (`task_name = "navigator"`, read-only,
  `max_iterations` small, e.g. 4) — denied `move`/`attack` via `allow:`
  (Phase A engine, again the natural first real use).
- `consult_navigator(from:, to:)` — a native tool following the exact
  `RunDSL#tool` + `#dispatch` pattern `inspect_room` pioneered, runs its own
  throwaway `Context`/`Registry` against the Phase H query tool, isolated
  from the caller's context (only one tool_call/result pair leaks back).
  Registered on Player, Judge, and Planner's registries.

### Phase J — Cross-session character memory

Closest to a clean port since nothing overlapping exists yet:

- `Boukensha::PlayerMemory` — `.boukensha/memory/<name>.jsonl` append-only
  raw records + `<name>.md` bounded prose digest. Use the **open-append-close**
  pattern everywhere (never hold a file handle open) — this is a documented
  real bug from Phase D/B on this exact codebase (Windows can't clean up a
  handle another process holds open).
- `Tasks::Chronicler < Tasks::Base` (`task_name = "chronicler"`, zero tools
  by design). `Boukensha.run_chronicler` rewrites the digest under fixed
  headers (Discoveries / Mistakes / Strategies / Open threads).
- Memory reaches the Player **only through the Planner** (a
  `player_memory:` kwarg on `run_planner`) — the Player's own prompt stays
  untouched, keeping the existing, already-tested Player path stable.
- Flush points: every Judge `:replan`/`:flag` verdict, plus `/clear`,
  `/exit`, and EOF in the `Repl` (same boundaries the compactor and
  telemetry already care about). `memory.enabled: false` by default.

### Settings & permissions

- `settings.yaml` gains `tasks: planner:`, `tasks: judge:`, `tasks:
  navigator:`, `tasks: chronicler:` blocks (provider/model per task, same
  shape as the existing `tasks: player:`).
- This is the moment to **turn on `Boukensha::Permissions`** (`allow:`) for
  the new tasks — it's already built and tested (Phase A) but not wired
  into the live profile; Judge/Navigator need it for real, not as a demo.

